@Tutorials(name: "Modeling Failures") {
    @Intro(title: "Modeling Failures Without Exceptions") {
        Swift's `throws` collapses every failure into a single channel — one
        `Error` thrown up the call stack, with no way to express "this can fail
        in two distinct ways" or "collect every problem instead of stopping at
        the first." This library instead models failure explicitly as data,
        and offers four types to pick from depending on what you need to say:
        `Optional` when you only need to know *whether* a value exists,
        `Result` when you need to say *why* it failed, `Either` when the
        failure type shouldn't be forced to conform to `Error`, and
        `Validation` when you need to report *every* failure at once instead
        of just the first one encountered.
    }

    @Chapter(name: "From Optional to Validation") {
        @Image(source: "modeling-failures-chapter.svg", alt: "Optional leads to Result, which leads to Either, which leads to Validation")

        Follow one small, concrete example — validating a username — as its
        requirements grow step by step, and watch which type this library
        reaches for at each stage.

        @TutorialReference(tutorial: "doc:ModelingFailures")
    }
}
